<section xml:id="sec-mod2-study-guide">
  <title>Module 2: Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
  <p>Zorn §1.7&#x2013;1.9 — Estimated time: 50 minutes</p>

    <p>
      This guide accompanies three sections of Zorn's
      <em>Understanding Real Analysis</em>. Section 1.1 is listed in
      the module syllabus as background reading: skim it to orient
      yourself to the number systems and to remind yourself of the proof
      that <m>\sqrt{2} \notin \Q</m>, which is a model contradiction
      argument. The active reading and guided questions below begin
      with Section 1.7, where the analytic content starts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read each section before working its questions. The questions are
      designed to be answered with the text open in front of you.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 1.7: ABSOLUTE VALUE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-sg-17-intro">
    <title>Section 1.7: Absolute Values (Zorn pp. 62&#x2013;65)</title>
    <p>
      This section introduces absolute value as a distance function and
      establishes the properties you will use in every subsequent module.
      Pay particular attention to Theorem 1.23(c) (which converts an
      absolute value inequality into an interval) and Theorem 1.24
      (the Triangle Inequality).
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-17-1">
    <title>Absolute Value as Distance</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each expression, (i) compute the value and (ii) describe
        in one sentence what the expression measures geometrically on
        the number line.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>|{-3} - 5|</m></li>
          <li><m>|x - 2|</m> where <m>x = 7</m></li>
          <li><m>|x - 2|</m> for a general <m>x \in \R</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>|{-3} - 5| = |{-8}| = 8</m>; this is the distance
        between <m>-3</m> and <m>5</m> on the number line.
        (b) <m>|7 - 2| = 5</m>; the distance from <m>7</m> to
        <m>2</m>.
        (c) <m>|x - 2|</m> is the distance from <m>x</m> to
        <m>2</m>, regardless of which side of <m>2</m> the point
        <m>x</m> lies on.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-17-2">
    <title>Converting Absolute Value Inequalities</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Theorem 1.23(c) says: <m>|x| \lt k \iff -k \lt x \lt k</m>
        (for <m>k \gt 0</m>).
        <!-- TODO(quinn): sentence added in #10 (math fixes); confirm or replace. -->
        The non-strict form <m>|x| \leq k \iff -k \leq x \leq k</m>
        (for <m>k \geq 0</m>) also holds, since <m>|x|</m> is the larger
        of <m>x</m> and <m>-x</m>. Use these to rewrite each inequality
        as a simple interval, then verify your answer geometrically
        by identifying the center and radius of the interval.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>|x - 3| \lt 2</m></li>
          <li><m>|x + 1| \leq 4</m></li>
          <li><m>|2x - 5| \lt 1</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>1 \lt x \lt 5</m>; center <m>3</m>, radius <m>2</m>.
        (b) By the non-strict form, <m>-4 \leq x + 1 \leq 4</m>, so
        <m>-5 \leq x \leq 3</m>; center <m>-1</m>, radius <m>4</m>.
        (c) <m>|2x - 5| \lt 1 \Rightarrow -1 \lt 2x - 5 \lt 1
        \Rightarrow 4 \lt 2x \lt 6 \Rightarrow 2 \lt x \lt 3</m>;
        center <m>5/2</m>, radius <m>1/2</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-17-3">
    <title>Key Lemma for the Triangle Inequality</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        One step in Zorn's proof of the Triangle Inequality uses
        the fact that for any real number <m>x</m>,
        <md>-|x| \leq x \leq |x|.</md>
        Prove this directly from the definition of absolute value
        (considering the cases <m>x \geq 0</m> and <m>x \lt 0</m>
        separately).
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        When <m>x \geq 0</m>: <m>|x| = x</m>, so the right inequality
        is <m>x \leq x</m> (trivially true) and the left inequality is
        <m>-x \leq x</m> (true since <m>x \geq 0</m>). Handle
        <m>x \lt 0</m> analogously.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-17-4">
    <title>Applying the Triangle Inequality</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a, b, c \in \R</m>. Apply the Triangle Inequality
        to the expression <m>a - c = (a - b) + (b - c)</m> to
        obtain an upper bound on <m>|a - c|</m> in terms of
        <m>|a - b|</m> and <m>|b - c|</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This result is sometimes called the <em>metric inequality</em>
        for <m>\R</m>: the distance from <m>a</m> to <m>c</m> is at
        most the sum of the distances from <m>a</m> to <m>b</m> and
        from <m>b</m> to <m>c</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>|a - c| = |(a - b) + (b - c)| \leq |a - b| + |b - c|</m>
        by the Triangle Inequality applied with <m>x = a - b</m>
        and <m>y = b - c</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 1.8: BOUNDS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-sg-18-intro">
    <title>Section 1.8: Bounds (Zorn pp. 67&#x2013;73)</title>
    <p>
      This section introduces the vocabulary of upper and lower bounds
      and defines the supremum (least upper bound) and infimum
      (greatest lower bound). Two things to focus on: (1) the
      <em>definition</em> of supremum requires two conditions, and both
      matter; (2) the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization of the supremum
      is the key proof technique you will use repeatedly.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-18-1">
    <title>Identifying Bounds</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each set, determine whether it is bounded above, bounded
        below, or both. If it is bounded above, give an example of an
        upper bound (not necessarily the least one).
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\{n^2 : n \in \N\}</m></li>
          <li><m>\left\{\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n} : n \in \N\right\}</m></li>
          <li><m>\{x \in \R : x^2 \leq 9\}</m></li>
          <li><m>\left\{1 - \dfrac{1}{n} : n \in \N\right\}</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Bounded below by 1 (or 0); not bounded above.
        (b) Bounded above and below: all elements satisfy
        <m>|(-1)^n/n| = 1/n \leq 1</m>, so <m>-1</m> is a lower
        bound and <m>1</m> is an upper bound.
        (c) Bounded above by <m>3</m> and below by <m>-3</m>; this
        is the interval <m>[-3, 3]</m>.
        (d) Bounded above by <m>1</m> and below by <m>0</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-18-2">
    <title>Finding Suprema and Infima</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each set in the previous exercise that is bounded above,
        identify <m>\sup S</m>. For each set that is bounded below,
        identify <m>\inf S</m>. (Do not prove your answers yet;
        just identify them and briefly explain your reasoning.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>\inf\{n^2\} = 1</m> (achieved at <m>n = 1</m>); no supremum.
        (b) <m>\sup = 1/2</m> (at <m>n = 2</m>, even);
        <m>\inf = -1</m> (at <m>n = 1</m>, odd).
        (c) <m>\sup = 3</m> and <m>\inf = -3</m>, both achieved.
        (d) <m>\sup = 1</m> (limit, never achieved); <m>\inf = 0</m>
        (at <m>n=1</m>, giving <m>0</m>, which is achieved).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-18-3">
    <title>The Two Conditions for Supremum</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        According to Zorn's definition, a real number <m>s</m> equals
        <m>\sup S</m> if and only if two conditions hold. State both
        conditions in your own words (not just symbols), and explain
        why <em>both</em> are needed. In particular: why is it not
        enough to say that <m>s</m> is an upper bound of <m>S</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-18-4">
    <title>The <m>\varepsilon</m>-Characterization of the Supremum</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Zorn states (or you can derive) the following equivalent
        condition: <m>s = \sup S</m> if and only if
        <ol marker="(i)">
          <li><m>s</m> is an upper bound of <m>S</m>, and</li>
          <li>
            for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, there exists
            <m>x \in S</m> with <m>s - \varepsilon \lt x \leq s</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
        Condition (i) is the same as in the definition; condition (ii)
        replaces the definition's second condition. Together, (i) and
        (ii) are called the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization.
        In plain English, what does condition (ii) say about how
        <m>s</m> relates to the elements of <m>S</m>?
        Why does it guarantee that no number smaller than <m>s</m>
        can be an upper bound?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- TODO(quinn): paragraph added in #10 (math fixes); confirm or replace. Check that the definition matches Zorn's in Section 1.8. -->
  <p>
    You now have two equivalent ways to show <m>s = \sup S</m>.
    The definition: <m>s</m> is an
    upper bound of <m>S</m>, and <m>s \leq u</m> for every upper bound
    <m>u</m> of <m>S</m>. The <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization:
    <m>s</m> is an upper bound of <m>S</m>, and for every
    <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there exists <m>x \in S</m> with
    <m>s - \varepsilon \lt x \leq s</m>. Exercises say which one to
    verify.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 1.9: COMPLETENESS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-sg-19-intro">
    <title>Section 1.9: Completeness (Zorn pp. 74&#x2013;82)</title>
    <p>
      Section 1.9 delivers the central axiom of real analysis. Read
      this section carefully. The key question to keep in mind is:
      <em>what would go wrong if we tried to do analysis in
      <m>\Q</m> instead of <m>\R</m>?</em>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-19-1">
    <title>Why <m>\Q</m> is Incomplete</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the set <m>T = \{x \in \Q : x^2 \lt 2\}</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Explain why <m>T</m> is nonempty and bounded above
            (as a subset of <m>\Q</m>).
          </li>
          <li>
            Explain why <m>T</m> has no supremum <em>in <m>\Q</m></em>.
            (Hint: whatever rational upper bound you propose, you can
            always find a closer rational that is still above
            <m>\sqrt{2}</m>&#x2014;or use the fact that
            <m>\sqrt{2} \notin \Q</m> directly.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Where does the supremum of <m>T</m> live? What is its value?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>T</m> is nonempty: <m>1 \in T</m> since <m>1^2 = 1 \lt 2</m>.
        It is bounded above in <m>\Q</m>: for example, <m>2 \in \Q</m>
        and <m>2^2 = 4 \gt 2</m>, so <m>x \lt 2</m> for all <m>x \in T</m>.
        (b) Suppose <m>q \in \Q</m> were a supremum of <m>T</m>.
        Since <m>1 \in T</m>, <m>q \geq 1</m>.
        If <m>q^2 \lt 2</m>, we can find a rational <m>r</m> with
        <m>q \lt r \lt \sqrt{2}</m>; since <m>0 \lt r \lt \sqrt{2}</m>,
        <m>r^2 \lt 2</m>, so <m>r</m> is an element of <m>T</m> greater
        than <m>q</m>, contradicting that <m>q</m> is an upper bound.
        So <m>q^2 \geq 2</m>.
        But <m>q^2 = 2</m> would mean <m>q = \sqrt{2} \notin \Q</m>
        (as <m>q \gt 0</m>), contradiction. And if <m>q^2 \gt 2</m>,
        we can find a rational <m>r</m> strictly between
        <m>\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>q</m>. Since <m>r \gt \sqrt{2} \gt 0</m>,
        we have <m>r^2 \gt 2 \gt x^2</m>, and hence <m>x \lt r</m>, for
        every <m>x \in T</m>. So <m>r</m> is a rational upper bound of
        <m>T</m> smaller than <m>q</m>, contradicting <m>q</m> being the
        <em>least</em> upper bound. (Both steps use the density of
        <m>\Q</m> in <m>\R</m>, stated in
        <xref ref="par-mod2-br-density" text="custom">the Bridge Reading</xref>.)
        (c) The supremum is <m>\sqrt{2} \in \R \setminus \Q</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-19-2">
    <title>Stating the Completeness Axiom</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the Completeness Axiom (also called the Least Upper Bound
        Property) in your own words. Your statement should make clear:
        what kind of set the axiom applies to, and what the axiom
        guarantees.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then check: does your statement apply to the set <m>T</m> in
        the previous exercise? What does it tell you?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-19-3">
    <title>Completeness and the Archimedean Property</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Zorn uses the Completeness Axiom to prove the Archimedean
        Property of <m>\R</m>: for every <m>x \in \R</m>, there
        exists <m>n \in \N</m> with <m>n \gt x</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the proof (or sketch) in Zorn's text. What set is
        considered, and what does the Completeness Axiom apply to?
        Where is the contradiction reached? Write a two- or three-sentence
        summary of the argument in your own words.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-sg-19-4">
    <title>Using the Archimedean Property</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The Archimedean Property implies the following useful fact:
        for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, there exists
        <m>n \in \N</m> such that <m>\dfrac{1}{n} \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Explain why this follows from the Archimedean Property.
        (Hint: apply the Archimedean Property to the real number
        <m>1/\varepsilon</m>.)
      </p>
      <p>
        Why will this fact be useful when we work with sequences in
        Module 4?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        By the Archimedean Property applied to <m>x = 1/\varepsilon</m>,
        there exists <m>n \in \N</m> with <m>n \gt 1/\varepsilon</m>.
        Since <m>n \gt 0</m>, this gives <m>1/n \lt \varepsilon</m>.
        In Module 4, this will allow us to find, for any
        <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, an index <m>N</m> beyond which
        terms of sequences like <m>1/n</m> lie within <m>\varepsilon</m>
        of their limit.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

</section>
